es, fixture accessors, fake-script formatters, state/mailbox accessors, output validators, wait predicates, and nonce parsing. The changed tests validate nonce-substring confirmation and negative cases without adding mixed production behavior. |\n| `src-tauri/tests/wu_d_proactive_wake_integration.rs` | Fixture methods, proactive wake integration tests including #44 backlog/reap coverage, command builders, claim seeders, assertion helpers, script formatter, wait/poll helpers, identity mapper, timestamp parser, and path/TOML formatters | Source lines 72-1451; diff hunks 384-1174 | exactly one per symbol: orchestration, formatter, mapper, accessor, parser, validator, predicate, or filter | LOW | The #44 backlog test is test orchestration over named fixture seeders, mailbox aging, live/dead claim seeders, command runners, and assertions. Helper functions remain split: claim seeders orchestrate mailbox state setup; `current_process_identity`/`identity` map identity shapes; wait helpers poll predicates; script/path/TOML helpers format fixture text. Test-only splitting was not treated as runtime behavior change. |\n\n## Multi-Classifier Findings\n\n| ID | Path | Function / symbol | Categories mixed | Evidence | Suggested split | Blocking or residual | Finding origin | Domain relation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n## Residual Ambiguity / Stop-Condition Notes\n\nNo HIGH `multi-classifier function` findings were identified in the touched-file inventory.\n\nLarge touched Rust modules were inventoried whole-file by executable named function bodies, with focused body reads for the #44 sweep-hardening surface and changed hunks plus whole-file function discovery for adjacent pre-existing functions. Inline closures used as trivial iterator predicates/mappers or helper-dispatch arguments were reviewed as part of their containing body evidence; no closure introduced an independent body with two A1 categories.\n\n`src-tauri/src/lib.rs` is touched but contains only module